WEBVTT

00:00:00.035 --> 00:00:02.125
Picture the inquiry tracker from Lesson 1.

00:00:02.508 --> 00:00:05.956
Its owner says: "Requests are in too many places, I forget

00:00:05.991 --> 00:00:09.938
to respond, and I need help." That sentence is valuable,

00:00:10.263 --> 00:00:12.562
but it contains several possible products.

00:00:12.887 --> 00:00:16.277
We could build a shared inbox, a spreadsheet, an automated

00:00:16.324 --> 00:00:18.866
email service, or an inquiry dashboard.

00:00:19.377 --> 00:00:22.256
Your first job is to express the real problem in your own

00:00:22.314 --> 00:00:22.860
language.

00:00:23.185 --> 00:00:27.167
Include frustration, examples, constraints, and things you

00:00:27.237 --> 00:00:28.224
have not decided.

00:00:28.607 --> 00:00:30.987
You do not need to sort it into the perfect order.

00:00:31.497 --> 00:00:34.040
Ask the agent to hold its response until you say you are

00:00:34.075 --> 00:00:34.528
finished.

00:00:34.823 --> 00:00:37.784
Once you finish, ask the agent to organize the material

00:00:37.853 --> 00:00:42.509
into a goal, people, desired experience, constraints,

00:00:42.683 --> 00:00:47.002
known facts, assumptions, questions, and non-goals.

00:00:47.606 --> 00:00:50.822
A non-goal is something the project intentionally does not

00:00:50.857 --> 00:00:51.379
attempt.

00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:55.303
This organized response is a readback: a statement of what

00:00:55.338 --> 00:00:56.685
the agent believes you meant.

00:00:57.068 --> 00:00:58.868
It is not yet your architecture.

00:00:59.251 --> 00:01:01.004
It gives you a surface to correct.

00:01:01.445 --> 00:01:04.382
Tell the agent, "Repeat this back before choosing tools or

00:01:04.429 --> 00:01:05.125
writing code.

00:01:05.567 --> 00:01:08.260
Point out contradictions and wait for my correction."

00:01:08.579 --> 00:01:11.737
Compare "make a dashboard" with a description of an

00:01:11.818 --> 00:01:15.743
observable journey: "An event organizer submits a form.

00:01:16.068 --> 00:01:19.957
The performer opens a private list, sees a new inquiry,

00:01:20.108 --> 00:01:23.266
and marks it handled after replying." The second

00:01:23.324 --> 00:01:27.086
description names a person, an action, and a result.

00:01:27.597 --> 00:01:30.696
If the agent adds "the system automatically sends a

00:01:30.766 --> 00:01:34.063
reply," you can say, "No, I will write the response

00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:37.837
myself." That correction is easy before the agent chooses

00:01:37.883 --> 00:01:40.542
a mail provider and builds an automatic sender.

00:01:40.877 --> 00:01:44.662
A fact might be: "There is already a website form," if

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:46.079
someone has inspected it.

00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:50.479
An assumption might be: "The form stores every message,"

00:01:50.688 --> 00:01:51.849
if no one has checked.

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:55.936
An unknown might be: "Can an inquiry be submitted without

00:01:55.970 --> 00:01:59.523
an email address?" This distinction is not a test of your

00:01:59.581 --> 00:02:00.068
memory.

00:02:00.393 --> 00:02:03.888
It is a way to make uncertainty visible so the team can

00:02:03.958 --> 00:02:05.525
research the right things.

00:02:06.222 --> 00:02:09.972
Contradictions deserve attention too: asking for automatic

00:02:10.030 --> 00:02:13.385
outreach and also saying no messages should leave without

00:02:13.408 --> 00:02:15.335
approval needs clarification.

00:02:15.677 --> 00:02:19.137
Read the proposed journey and ask: does this describe what

00:02:19.184 --> 00:02:20.763
I want the user to experience?

00:02:21.204 --> 00:02:22.725
Correct it in ordinary language.

00:02:23.166 --> 00:02:25.325
When it is right, explicitly say so.

00:02:25.836 --> 00:02:28.762
This is an alignment gate, a check that the shared

00:02:28.808 --> 00:02:30.631
understanding is ready for design.

00:02:31.072 --> 00:02:33.661
The gate applies when you are still deciding what to

00:02:33.731 --> 00:02:36.715
build; it should not make you repeat yourself when you

00:02:36.796 --> 00:02:39.548
have already agreed and authorized a later task.

00:02:40.244 --> 00:02:43.356
Save the agreed readback so the next session can find it.

00:02:43.692 --> 00:02:46.896
Try: "I am going to describe the idea in pieces.

00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:49.485
Hold the context until I say done.

00:02:49.927 --> 00:02:54.327
Then repeat back the goal, people, observable journey,

00:02:54.652 --> 00:02:59.447
constraints, verified facts, assumptions, unknowns, and

00:02:59.516 --> 00:03:00.294
non-goals.

00:03:00.805 --> 00:03:02.593
Identify contradictions.

00:03:03.034 --> 00:03:05.043
Do not choose the architecture yet.

00:03:05.426 --> 00:03:08.804
Wait for my corrections." The prompt is a starting point,

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:10.081
not magic words.

00:03:10.407 --> 00:03:13.170
The important pattern is that you retain the authority to

00:03:13.251 --> 00:03:15.585
correct the agent's interpretation before an

00:03:15.643 --> 00:03:17.709
interpretation becomes implementation.

00:03:18.002 --> 00:03:20.475
Here is what the first few turns might sound like.

00:03:20.858 --> 00:03:24.712
The owner says, "I lose event inquiries and want one list.

00:03:25.037 --> 00:03:28.718
I prefer to reply personally." The agent asks to hear the

00:03:28.776 --> 00:03:29.705
rest and waits.

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:32.932
The owner explains that requests arrive through a website

00:03:33.001 --> 00:03:36.252
and sometimes through email, that only the owner should

00:03:36.287 --> 00:03:39.898
see contact details, and that the first version can start

00:03:39.956 --> 00:03:40.861
with the website.

00:03:41.303 --> 00:03:43.706
The agent then summarizes those points.

00:03:44.147 --> 00:03:47.375
It does not quietly interpret "help me reply" as

00:03:47.456 --> 00:03:49.395
permission to send an automatic message.

00:03:49.724 --> 00:03:53.277
The agent says, "The system imports all your email into a

00:03:53.346 --> 00:03:56.806
shared dashboard." The owner corrects it: "Only the

00:03:56.864 --> 00:04:00.219
website form is in the first version; I have not decided

00:04:00.278 --> 00:04:01.264
on email imports.

00:04:01.589 --> 00:04:05.479
The list is private, not shared." A good agent updates the

00:04:05.549 --> 00:04:08.068
readback and keeps those two distinctions visible.

00:04:08.509 --> 00:04:11.633
If the owner says "That is right," capture the resulting

00:04:11.691 --> 00:04:12.132
journey.

00:04:12.515 --> 00:04:14.883
If the owner says "Almost," keep listening.

00:04:15.266 --> 00:04:18.018
A correction is a normal part of the process, not a

00:04:18.088 --> 00:04:20.073
failure of the owner to prompt perfectly.

00:04:20.406 --> 00:04:23.576
Write the journey as a short sequence: an organizer opens

00:04:23.611 --> 00:04:26.885
the public form; they enter contact and event details;

00:04:27.268 --> 00:04:31.297
they submit; the system either confirms a successful save

00:04:31.575 --> 00:04:34.942
or explains a failure; the owner later opens a private

00:04:34.977 --> 00:04:38.077
list; the owner reviews the inquiry and changes its

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:38.715
status.

00:04:39.156 --> 00:04:42.152
Each sentence describes behavior that someone can later

00:04:42.210 --> 00:04:42.628
check.

00:04:43.232 --> 00:04:46.320
Ask what happens if the user abandons the form, if

00:04:46.389 --> 00:04:50.186
required contact details are missing, and if saving fails.

00:04:50.569 --> 00:04:53.646
You need not decide every implementation detail at this

00:04:53.704 --> 00:04:54.064
point.

00:04:54.395 --> 00:04:57.646
Imagine the owner says, "The website probably saves

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:00.780
submissions." Mark that as an assumption until someone

00:05:00.815 --> 00:05:02.034
checks the actual system.

00:05:02.638 --> 00:05:05.958
If the agent finds a form in the repository, it can report

00:05:05.993 --> 00:05:07.352
that the form code exists.

00:05:07.793 --> 00:05:11.275
It cannot conclude, just from the file, that the live site

00:05:11.345 --> 00:05:13.168
saves submissions successfully.

00:05:13.679 --> 00:05:16.221
Ask it to describe precisely what it saw.

00:05:16.732 --> 00:05:20.192
This habit makes later research efficient: you can target

00:05:20.227 --> 00:05:22.537
the uncertain claims that would materially affect the

00:05:22.595 --> 00:05:23.326
architecture.

00:05:23.661 --> 00:05:27.388
An owner can say, "I want this to feel like a tidy inbox,"

00:05:27.713 --> 00:05:30.534
while also saying they do not want a copy of their email.

00:05:31.231 --> 00:05:34.272
The phrase conveys the experience but not the full feature

00:05:34.307 --> 00:05:34.714
list.

00:05:35.155 --> 00:05:38.011
Ask the agent to translate the metaphor into observable

00:05:38.046 --> 00:05:40.844
behavior and identify choices that remain.

00:05:41.227 --> 00:05:43.433
The owner decides what the metaphor means.

00:05:43.758 --> 00:05:46.347
This is particularly useful when an idea contains

00:05:46.405 --> 00:05:50.434
examples, comparisons, or partial solutions that could

00:05:50.469 --> 00:05:52.175
otherwise become hidden requirements.

00:05:52.551 --> 00:05:55.047
The output of this stage can be a small document or

00:05:55.117 --> 00:05:59.180
section: purpose, people, agreed journey,

00:05:59.622 --> 00:06:04.451
constraints, non-goals, verified facts, assumptions,

00:06:04.834 --> 00:06:06.437
and outstanding questions.

00:06:06.878 --> 00:06:10.500
Add a date and a clear status such as agreed or still

00:06:10.546 --> 00:06:11.405
being discussed.

00:06:12.009 --> 00:06:14.029
Keep it accessible to future sessions.

00:06:14.470 --> 00:06:17.849
Later, when the system changes, update or supersede the

00:06:17.907 --> 00:06:21.135
relevant decision rather than rely on someone remembering

00:06:21.169 --> 00:06:21.878
what was said.

00:06:22.574 --> 00:06:25.117
Now you have something concrete enough to research and

00:06:25.152 --> 00:06:25.674
design.

00:06:26.018 --> 00:06:28.921
You can also do the mirror exercise with a friend or by

00:06:28.990 --> 00:06:30.407
reading your own notes aloud.

00:06:30.917 --> 00:06:33.565
Ask the listener to describe the desired experience

00:06:33.599 --> 00:06:35.085
without naming a technology.

00:06:35.527 --> 00:06:38.870
If they cannot tell whether the owner manually replies or

00:06:38.928 --> 00:06:41.831
the software sends messages, add that distinction.

00:06:42.272 --> 00:06:45.129
If they ask how long records remain available and you do

00:06:45.210 --> 00:06:47.729
not know, mark it as an open decision.

00:06:48.112 --> 00:06:51.084
The purpose is shared understanding, not proving how much

00:06:51.166 --> 00:06:52.942
technical terminology you know.

00:06:53.545 --> 00:06:55.473
You can move forward when the central journey and

00:06:55.519 --> 00:06:58.572
decision-changing boundaries are clear, and continue to

00:06:58.619 --> 00:07:01.347
resolve smaller questions at the appropriate stage.
